WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.162
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4400.

2
00:00:01.162 --> 00:00:02.325
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:02.712
<v DOUGLAS MUNGIN>Alright.

4
00:00:02.712 --> 00:00:12.013
Today is January twenty-third, 2015 and I’m Douglas Mungin representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

5
00:00:12.013 --> 00:00:19.762
We are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum with Bill Shearman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

6
00:00:19.762 --> 00:00:28.288
And to kick that off, we are holding Mossville oral history day here at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

7
00:00:28.288 --> 00:00:29.838
I’m here today with . . .

8
00:00:29.838 --> 00:00:31.000
<v KEVIN FONDEL>Kevin Fondel.

9
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.718
<v MUNGIN>Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

10
00:00:33.718 --> 00:00:37.988
This is going to be a short interview, about fifteen minutes,

11
00:00:37.988 --> 00:00:44.588
and we will likely be in touch with you later to set up a much longer interview.

12
00:00:44.588 --> 00:00:44.976
Alright?

13
00:00:44.976 --> 00:00:47.694
Could you please state your full name?

14
00:00:47.694 --> 00:00:49.247
<v FONDEL>Kevin Joseph Fondel, Senior.

15
00:00:49.247 --> 00:00:51.965
<v MUNGIN>And when and where were you born?

16
00:00:51.965 --> 00:00:58.176
<v FONDEL>I was born in Lake Charles, Louisiana but my grandparents bought property in Mossville in 1956.

17
00:00:58.176 --> 00:01:02.447
And we’ve been having the property in our family since then.

18
00:01:02.447 --> 00:01:02.835
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

19
00:01:02.835 --> 00:01:04.000
Since 1956?

20
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:04.663
<v FONDEL>Since 1956.

21
00:01:04.663 --> 00:01:06.984
<v MUNGIN>And when did you come to Mossville?

22
00:01:06.984 --> 00:01:12.620
<v FONDEL>Well I came . . . I moved to Mossville in 1980 when I . . . Well, I didn’t graduate from college.

23
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:20.246
When I left college, came back, started working at Citco Refinery, we had the property in Mossville on Michigan Avenue and Third Street.

24
00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:25.551
And it was not developed so we cleared it off and put mobile home on it.

25
00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:25.882
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

26
00:01:25.882 --> 00:01:28.535
<v FONDEL>And it was me and my three brothers.

27
00:01:28.535 --> 00:01:31.519
<v MUNGIN>And so what brought you to Mossville in 1980?

28
00:01:31.519 --> 00:01:34.171
So you got . . . You started working for Citgo . . .

29
00:01:34.171 --> 00:01:35.166
<v FONDEL>Free property [laughs].

30
00:01:35.166 --> 00:01:41.465
Yeah it was in . . . The property was already in our family and nobody had ever done anything with it.

31
00:01:41.465 --> 00:01:44.449
So when I stared working I just . . . my grandmother

32
00:01:44.449 --> 00:01:48.428
<c.p>say well, “Hey we got this property if you want to move.”

33
00:01:48.428 --> 00:01:55.059
And then I was working on this side of the bridge so I had to fight the traffic every morning.

34
00:01:55.059 --> 00:02:06.000
So it was just advantageous that I bought the . . . that I moved something on that property and drove . . . I didn’t have to deal with the bridge every morning to get to work.

35
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.638
<v MUNGIN>And what were your parents’ names?

36
00:02:08.638 --> 00:02:12.595
<v FONDEL>My parents’ name is Marion Fondel and Ruth Fondel.

37
00:02:12.595 --> 00:02:15.233
<v MUNGIN>And what was your grandparents’ name?

38
00:02:15.233 --> 00:02:19.629
<v FONDEL>My grandparents’ name is Jessie Paige and Georgie Mae Paige.

39
00:02:19.629 --> 00:02:24.905
<v MUNGIN>And were they original . . . Part of the original fourteen families of Mossville?

40
00:02:24.905 --> 00:02:31.940
<v FONDEL>No, I don’t think . . . no, my family originated from . . . My dad is original from Lake Charles.

41
00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:36.776
They have a funeral home and all that in Lake Charles.

42
00:02:36.776 --> 00:02:44.690
But my . . . It’s actually my mother’s mom and dad that bought this property, and they’re from Natchitoches, Louisiana.

43
00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:46.888
<v MUNGIN>And what are their names?

44
00:02:46.888 --> 00:02:47.767
<v FONDEL>Georgie Paige . . .

45
00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:48.647
<v MUNGIN>Georgie Paige.

46
00:02:48.647 --> 00:02:50.405
That’s the Georgie Paige.

47
00:02:50.405 --> 00:02:51.724
<v FONDEL>And Jessie Paige.

48
00:02:51.724 --> 00:02:55.681
That was my mom’s parents actually bought this property.

49
00:02:55.681 --> 00:02:57.000
<v MUNGIN>In 1956?

50
00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:57.655
<v FONDEL>In 1956.

51
00:02:57.655 --> 00:02:59.946
<v MUNGIN>Why did they buy property in Mossville?

52
00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:05.512
<v FONDEL>Well my grandfather used to work for Dunham Price and he . . . That was right off Trousdale Road.

53
00:03:05.512 --> 00:03:06.494
<v MUNGIN>What’s Dunham Price?

54
00:03:06.494 --> 00:03:07.804
<v FONDEL>It’s a cement company.

55
00:03:07.804 --> 00:03:18.280
And I think from what they told me that he was . . . Bought the property there because he was working over there and he was going eventually build him a house over here,

56
00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.571
but he never got around to it.

57
00:03:20.571 --> 00:03:22.863
They end up staying in Lake Charles.

58
00:03:22.863 --> 00:03:26.792
<v MUNGIN>Why didn’t he ever get a chance to come around to it?

59
00:03:26.792 --> 00:03:33.667
<v FONDEL>Well because we actually grew up on Calcasieu Street in Lake Charles and we got his . . . my mother . . . In those days,

60
00:03:33.667 --> 00:03:36.286
our families was more everybody stay close together.

61
00:03:36.286 --> 00:03:39.232
And on that street we got like four houses.

62
00:03:39.232 --> 00:03:43.815
My grandparents have a house there, my mom and dad have a house there,

63
00:03:43.815 --> 00:03:49.054
my auntie has a house there, and I had another auntie had a house further down.

64
00:03:49.054 --> 00:03:52.000
So it was like four families living there.

65
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.000
<v MUNGIN>Who were your aunts?

66
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.000
<v FONDEL>My aunt was Lillie Mae Johnson and Willie Mae Johnson.

67
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.500
Everybody in my family name got a Mae in it [laughs].

68
00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.500
Some kind of way.

69
00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.500
<v MUNGIN>How long back does that go?

70
00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:17.000
<v FONDEL>Well since I guess 1920s because I got Willie Mae, Lillie Mae, Ruthie Mae . . . So.

71
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.000
<v MUNGIN>How long was your family in Lake Charles?

72
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.500
Like when did they first come to Lake Charles?

73
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:30.500
<v FONDEL>My dad’s family is at least the 1900s or earlier.

74
00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:40.500
I can’t . . . I’m not sure but his . . . The Fondel family actually had to be around 1900s on his father’s side.

75
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.000
Now his mother, it was a William.

76
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.500
She come from Saint Martinville.

77
00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:53.000
From the 1900s at least my father’s Fondel family’s been in Lake Charles.

78
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:53.500
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

79
00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:54.500
What’s your . . .

80
00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:55.500
<v FONDEL>Early 1900s.

81
00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.000
<v MUNGIN>What’s your earliest memory of Mossville?

82
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:05.184
<v FONDEL>Well right now it’s kind of like déjà vu because we had to clear all them pine trees.

83
00:05:05.184 --> 00:05:07.932
When we bought the property it was underdeveloped.

84
00:05:07.932 --> 00:05:10.337
Now watching them doing all that now,

85
00:05:10.337 --> 00:05:18.582
it’s kind of like brought me back to when me and my brothers was out there with our first adventures with a chainsaw [laughs].

86
00:05:18.582 --> 00:05:26.827
So and then after we cut it all down, it’s funny because this guy work with me had a trucking service that clear lots.

87
00:05:26.827 --> 00:05:32.323
Well out there we cut the stumps all the way to the ground and he said,

88
00:05:32.323 --> 00:05:37.133
“Man if y’all just left the trees I could have just pushed them over.”

89
00:05:37.133 --> 00:05:39.537
So it was like, oh man [laughs].

90
00:05:39.537 --> 00:05:42.629
If we did all . . . But we had fun though.

91
00:05:42.629 --> 00:05:45.721
We had a lot of fun clearing it off.

92
00:05:45.721 --> 00:05:49.500
<v MUNGIN>Did you ever come to Mossville when you were a kid?

93
00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:51.905
<v FONDEL>No, I . . . As a matter of fact,

94
00:05:51.905 --> 00:05:58.432
when my grandmother showed me the property it was actually the first time I actually was in this area.

95
00:05:58.432 --> 00:06:02.554
I never really . . . Growing up I pretty much stayed in Lake Charles.

96
00:06:02.554 --> 00:06:07.707
Didn’t really . . . No, I think we played a baseball game over here against the Pirates.

97
00:06:07.707 --> 00:06:08.395
Mossville Pirates.

98
00:06:08.395 --> 00:06:12.173
Because we used to have what they call Roy Campanella Field.

99
00:06:12.173 --> 00:06:17.327
And they had the sheriff league and it was from thirteen to fifteen years old.

100
00:06:17.327 --> 00:06:22.823
And they had a team from Mossville they used to . . . We used to play every year.

101
00:06:22.823 --> 00:06:24.197
<v MUNGIN>Were they any good?

102
00:06:24.197 --> 00:06:25.228
<v FONDEL>Yeah they were.

103
00:06:25.228 --> 00:06:31.068
And then we played . . . I played football at Washington High School and Westlake was in our division.

104
00:06:31.068 --> 00:06:31.755
Westlake High.

105
00:06:31.755 --> 00:06:34.503
And we played Westlake and we played Sulphur.

106
00:06:34.503 --> 00:06:38.626
But I never really came into the community until I moved here.

107
00:06:38.626 --> 00:06:40.000
<v MUNGIN>Why is that?

108
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:41.087
<v FONDEL>We just didn’t.

109
00:06:41.087 --> 00:06:43.987
I mean it’s just one of those things.

110
00:06:43.987 --> 00:06:49.785
Because like I say the property never was developed until we moved the trailer on it.

111
00:06:49.785 --> 00:06:54.134
<v MUNGIN>So what did the property look like when you first got there?

112
00:06:54.134 --> 00:06:55.221
<v FONDEL>Nothing but woods.

113
00:06:55.221 --> 00:06:55.584
<v MUNGIN>Yeah.

114
00:06:55.584 --> 00:06:57.396
<v FONDEL>A lot of trees; brush.

115
00:06:57.396 --> 00:07:01.745
<v MUNGIN>Was that how most of the properties looked when you got here?

116
00:07:01.745 --> 00:07:02.107
<v FONDEL>Yes.

117
00:07:02.107 --> 00:07:07.181
We had . . . They had a house on side of us that a guy owned,

118
00:07:07.181 --> 00:07:11.530
and then we had the Thibodeauxs on the property across from us.

119
00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:14.792
But behind us they had a house that were

120
00:07:14.792 --> 00:07:18.054
<c.p>there, but had torn . . . It was pretty much destroyed.

121
00:07:18.054 --> 00:07:25.302
I don’t know what happened to it, but all the way to the . . . Everything was still pretty much still wooded.

122
00:07:25.302 --> 00:07:34.000
They had a few houses on Third Street, but across from us is pretty much the same that . . . Like it was in 1980.

123
00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.299
<v MUNGIN>Are your neighbors still the same folks?

124
00:07:36.299 --> 00:07:38.599
<v FONDEL>No. They . . . Everybody moved out around ’96.

125
00:07:38.599 --> 00:07:43.197
<v MUNGIN>So who were your neighbors when you first kind of moved into that area?

126
00:07:43.197 --> 00:07:44.182
<v FONDEL>The Thibodeaux family.

127
00:07:44.182 --> 00:07:46.810
It was like six girls and one guy.

128
00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:48.124
He has the barbershop.

129
00:07:48.124 --> 00:07:55.022
I can’t think of his first name now, but that’s the first family that I met when I was in Mossville.

130
00:07:55.022 --> 00:07:58.635
And they had . . . that’s pretty much . . . I can remember the Bryants.

131
00:07:58.635 --> 00:08:00.934
They had a lot of families here.

132
00:08:00.934 --> 00:08:07.504
That’s the thing why I kind of wanted to do this because we still own some . . . The property over there.

133
00:08:07.504 --> 00:08:11.117
And in ’96 they had the lawsuit and all with that.

134
00:08:11.117 --> 00:08:18.672
Then they bought a lot of people out, but my grandmother didn’t want to . . . She’s old school and she didn’t want to sell.

135
00:08:18.672 --> 00:08:24.255
Then when she passed away, my mom and dad they didn’t . . . They didn’t want to sell it.

136
00:08:24.255 --> 00:08:30.825
So we never got around to selling the property, but my little boy has a horse on the property now.

137
00:08:30.825 --> 00:08:38.708
And when we’re out there feeding the horse we always see old people that used to live in that area come by and say,

138
00:08:38.708 --> 00:08:41.007
“Man I sure miss being back here.”

139
00:08:41.007 --> 00:08:45.606
So and that’s kind of why I wanted to have a record of it.

140
00:08:45.606 --> 00:08:50.861
Because I’m . . . The way things going, eventually Sasol is going to buy us out too, so.

141
00:08:50.861 --> 00:08:53.489
And I just want a record of that.

142
00:08:53.489 --> 00:09:01.372
We were there at one time because I know we’re not going to be able to drive back there once they buy it out.

143
00:09:01.372 --> 00:09:04.000
<v MUNGIN>So what do you think they miss?

144
00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:13.953
<v FONDEL>Well I mean it’s like I grew up on the street that my mom and dad still own a house there and that’s been fifty years.

145
00:09:13.953 --> 00:09:15.484
So it’s that community.

146
00:09:15.484 --> 00:09:20.077
People . . . A lot of people say, “Man y’all getting all this money,”

147
00:09:20.077 --> 00:09:25.819
and all this, but at some stage in your life money ain’t all that important.

148
00:09:25.819 --> 00:09:32.327
And then you got to relocate, and then you got to have new neighbors and all that.

149
00:09:32.327 --> 00:09:36.920
So back when I moved to Mossville man, everybody was like family.

150
00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:40.366
You could walk down the street, nobody bothered you.

151
00:09:40.366 --> 00:09:50.318
It’s not saying that it’s like that in Lake Charles now because Lake Charles still is a small community where you could still walk the streets.

152
00:09:50.318 --> 00:09:59.505
But it’s just that they grew up together, you familiar with people and now that they done moved off, they just don’t have that.

153
00:09:59.505 --> 00:10:02.568
It’s just . . . like you miss your best friend.

154
00:10:02.568 --> 00:10:06.396
A community is like something you just used to having.

155
00:10:06.396 --> 00:10:14.434
And I’m sad that it’s coming to this, but opportunity for people to get jobs and all that is important, too.

156
00:10:14.434 --> 00:10:23.622
They have other communities that are not as fortunate as we are to have industry come into . . . in they . . . but there are sacrifices, so.

157
00:10:23.622 --> 00:10:31.277
<v MUNGIN>And so what’s . . . so in 1980 was it . . . You said that it was kind of like a lot of woods,

158
00:10:31.277 --> 00:10:35.871
but it was kind of like a family kind of connection there.

159
00:10:35.871 --> 00:10:41.996
<v FONDEL>Yeah I mean it still was . . . It wasn’t totally developed, but they had people living there.

160
00:10:41.996 --> 00:10:49.652
But it wasn’t like you was in the city where you had . . . How every lot had a house on it.

161
00:10:49.652 --> 00:10:59.222
You had . . . it still had . . . they had . . . I’d say in the Bel Air [subdivision] we had about a good hundred people living in that area.

162
00:10:59.222 --> 00:11:03.815
Spread out over the whole . . . I guess it was like four streets;

163
00:11:03.815 --> 00:11:09.174
spread out over that because we had Thibodaux family, another Thibodaux family . . . It’s been

164
00:11:09.174 --> 00:11:15.682
<c.p>awhile so I can’t remember . . . I know the faces, but I can’t call the names of them.

165
00:11:15.682 --> 00:11:19.510
And you had the guy that drove the dump truck.

166
00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:27.166
I can’t . . . I mean it . . . I can’t really remember his name, but if I see him I know him. [Comeaux?].

167
00:11:27.166 --> 00:11:27.548
Comeaux.

168
00:11:27.548 --> 00:11:28.314
Comeaux family.

169
00:11:28.314 --> 00:11:33.290
And it was . . . And we had a couple little restaurants on the corner.

170
00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:36.353
It wasn’t . . . it was a small community still,

171
00:11:36.353 --> 00:11:41.712
but and like I say we had . . . we wasn’t . . . I’m not originally from Mossville,

172
00:11:41.712 --> 00:11:46.305
but I lived there for the . . . up until ’96 I moved there.

173
00:11:46.305 --> 00:11:52.047
I put the trailer in 1980, and then I moved . . . I got married, moved off.

174
00:11:52.047 --> 00:11:53.578
My brother stayed there.

175
00:11:53.578 --> 00:11:58.938
So somebody in my family stayed up there until I moved back in 1990.

176
00:11:58.938 --> 00:12:02.000
And then we moved away in ’96,’97.

177
00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:02.328
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

178
00:12:02.328 --> 00:12:06.597
Back tracking a little bit, so where did you go to elementary school?

179
00:12:06.597 --> 00:12:09.224
<v FONDEL>I went to elementary school at Cherry Street.

180
00:12:09.224 --> 00:12:09.881
<v MUNGIN>Cherry Street.

181
00:12:09.881 --> 00:12:11.851
And you went to Washington High?

182
00:12:11.851 --> 00:12:12.836
<v FONDEL>Washington High school.

183
00:12:12.836 --> 00:12:13.164
<v MUNGIN>Cool.

184
00:12:13.164 --> 00:12:14.806
Did you go to college?

185
00:12:14.806 --> 00:12:16.448
<v FONDEL>I went to Northeast University.

186
00:12:16.448 --> 00:12:18.418
Now it’s University of Louisiana Monroe.

187
00:12:18.418 --> 00:12:20.388
And then I went to McNeese.

188
00:12:20.388 --> 00:12:24.000
<v MUNGIN>Why did you go to . . . End up going to McNeese?

189
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.325
<v FONDEL>My momma . . . Well, that’s what happened.

190
00:12:26.325 --> 00:12:30.974
I ended up transferring to McNeese . . . No, actually I got the job.

191
00:12:30.974 --> 00:12:36.010
And then it was like I’m not going to go back to school.

192
00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:42.983
I started working and I went part-time to McNeese, but . . . and that’s what I keep telling my kids.

193
00:12:42.983 --> 00:12:50.732
That you want to get that education out the way because once you get started working, you say, “Oh yeah.

194
00:12:50.732 --> 00:12:51.894
I’m going back.

195
00:12:51.894 --> 00:12:53.056
I’m going back.”

196
00:12:53.056 --> 00:12:54.606
But you never do.

197
00:12:54.606 --> 00:12:58.480
You never do really go back and finish it up.

198
00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:05.066
<v MUNGIN>So when you got to Mossville, like what were some of the important places in this neighborhood?

199
00:13:05.066 --> 00:13:11.265
<v FONDEL>They used to have a . . . Perkins had the hamburger place on the corner right over there.

200
00:13:11.265 --> 00:13:13.977
And they had . . . Kojacks had a restaurant.

201
00:13:13.977 --> 00:13:15.914
Mr. Thibodeux had a barbershop.

202
00:13:15.914 --> 00:13:23.275
They had a . . . on the corner of Michigan and Third they had a . . . It was a car repair shop.

203
00:13:23.275 --> 00:13:31.023
And then across the street . . . I can’t remember the guy’s name, but he had a little restaurant and beauty shop.

204
00:13:31.023 --> 00:13:33.735
That was pretty much what we had.

205
00:13:33.735 --> 00:13:40.321
And after eight o’clock we . . . Pretty much we had to drive to Westlake or Sulphur for anything.

206
00:13:40.321 --> 00:13:44.195
And they had this community center here the whole time,

207
00:13:44.195 --> 00:13:50.007
which was real . . . we used to . . . I used to walk to a gospel community center.

208
00:13:50.007 --> 00:13:58.917
And it was kind of nice they had all this right here because the community swimming pool, and the gym, and all that.

209
00:13:58.917 --> 00:14:01.629
And of course they had the school.

210
00:14:01.629 --> 00:14:07.828
And back then they had . . . they actually had the . . . still had the stadium behind the school.

211
00:14:07.828 --> 00:14:11.702
Because I used to go walking around the stadium . . . exercise.

212
00:14:11.702 --> 00:14:12.089
<v MUNGIN>Yeah.

213
00:14:12.089 --> 00:14:16.351
<v FONDEL>So it was . . . Still had a lot going on back here.

214
00:14:16.351 --> 00:14:21.000
Of course they had Conoco and [Condea] Vista at that time.

215
00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:27.368
<v MUNGIN>So where did people . . . So who were some of the important community leaders in this area?

216
00:14:27.368 --> 00:14:29.756
<v FONDEL>Well I guess the Rigmaiden family.

217
00:14:29.756 --> 00:14:36.124
The guy that owned James Funeral Home, they used to call him the mayor of Mossville.

218
00:14:36.124 --> 00:14:39.308
That’s what my dad used to call him.

219
00:14:39.308 --> 00:14:41.696
I don’t remember his whole name.

220
00:14:41.696 --> 00:14:52.044
But they used to have . . . a guy I worked with that had a house right across from the school worked on the . . . for the sheriff department.

221
00:14:52.044 --> 00:14:52.840
Kenneth Lee.

222
00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:58.412
And he had a brother that worked . . . Lived right down the road over here.

223
00:14:58.412 --> 00:15:03.586
That was pretty much the people that I knew that’s still in Mossville.

224
00:15:03.586 --> 00:15:03.984
<v MUNGIN>Alright.

225
00:15:03.984 --> 00:15:06.372
So three more questions for you.

226
00:15:06.372 --> 00:15:08.760
What does home mean to you?

227
00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:13.138
<v FONDEL>Well it’s that place you can put your car in overdrive,

228
00:15:13.138 --> 00:15:21.496
on one of them nights you had a little bit too much to drink and it know it’s way home [laughs].

229
00:15:21.496 --> 00:15:28.660
Nah, what . . . To me home is a place that no matter what, you know you can always go.

230
00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:33.436
And that’s basically the reason why my grandmother never sold that property.

231
00:15:33.436 --> 00:15:42.988
Because she said at one point in time somebody in our family may need somewhere to stay and that property will always be there.

232
00:15:42.988 --> 00:15:47.366
And that’s the hard part about selling any type of property.

233
00:15:47.366 --> 00:15:49.356
Because my grandmother . . . Grandparents purchased

234
00:15:49.356 --> 00:15:52.540
<c.p>their property in 1956 for six hundred dollars.

235
00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:59.704
And so I mean you can go put a tent up and can’t nobody run you off it,

236
00:15:59.704 --> 00:16:06.072
but I mean it’s still . . . It’s not a mansion or whatever like that, but it’s yours.

237
00:16:06.072 --> 00:16:18.808
And nobody can . . . because you got people right now . . . I read in the paper they got a trailer park in Mossville they go to get . . . They got two weeks to get off.

238
00:16:18.808 --> 00:16:22.788
And them people don’t know where they going to go.

239
00:16:22.788 --> 00:16:32.738
And I think that’s the most important thing to me about home is that if you own it, can’t nobody force you off of it.

240
00:16:32.738 --> 00:16:34.728
Well, now yeah they can.

241
00:16:34.728 --> 00:16:39.902
I mean the government can do a lot of things, but theoretically [laughs].

242
00:16:39.902 --> 00:16:47.066
Because they got that eminent domain and all that other stuff that tells you you got to go.

243
00:16:47.066 --> 00:16:50.648
But to me, that’s what I think of home.

244
00:16:50.648 --> 00:16:56.618
And then . . . In where I grew up right now . . . A lot of people say this,

245
00:16:56.618 --> 00:16:59.802
but when I grew up everybody knew everybody.

246
00:16:59.802 --> 00:17:02.986
And if I did something down the street,

247
00:17:02.986 --> 00:17:14.130
by the time I got home I had got whipped all the way back home . . . Now these days they don’t believe in whooping their kids and all that,

248
00:17:14.130 --> 00:17:18.508
but you had disciplinary all the way back to your house.

249
00:17:18.508 --> 00:17:24.876
Your mom and dad . . . Everybody raised . . . They say it take a village to raise a child,

250
00:17:24.876 --> 00:17:27.264
well basically that’s what it was.

251
00:17:27.264 --> 00:17:29.254
And that’s what Mossville’s been.

252
00:17:29.254 --> 00:17:31.642
And I’m not . . . I’m an import.

253
00:17:31.642 --> 00:17:34.428
I come from somewhere else to Mossville.

254
00:17:34.428 --> 00:17:40.000
I didn’t originate from here, but I do respect what they have built.

255
00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:40.429
<v MUNGIN>Alright.

256
00:17:40.429 --> 00:17:43.429
So what does community mean to you?

257
00:17:43.429 --> 00:17:51.143
<v FONDEL>Pretty much the same thing, but on the community it’s larger . . . Okay when you look at a community,

258
00:17:51.143 --> 00:17:54.571
Sasol, Conoco is a part of this community.

259
00:17:54.571 --> 00:17:57.571
A very important part of this community.

260
00:17:57.571 --> 00:18:02.714
Without them, there probably wouldn’t be a community because jobs are important.

261
00:18:02.714 --> 00:18:06.143
I mean I work at . . . for L&amp;G plant.

262
00:18:06.143 --> 00:18:11.714
So if you don’t have jobs, you don’t have the means for a

263
00:18:11.714 --> 00:18:14.286
<c.p>person to make a living then.

264
00:18:14.286 --> 00:18:19.857
So . . . but there got to be that medium where everybody can be satisfied.

265
00:18:19.857 --> 00:18:22.000
And that’s what I think.

266
00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:28.429
And Lake Charles . . . and this surrounding everything . . . The Lake Charles surrounding community is pretty decent.

267
00:18:28.429 --> 00:18:31.000
I mean I have no complaints.

268
00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:35.714
It’s a good community and it’s a good place to live.

269
00:18:35.714 --> 00:18:37.429
I’ve enjoyed it, so.

270
00:18:37.429 --> 00:18:40.429
Now we . . . I’m not against all this.

271
00:18:40.429 --> 00:18:46.000
I’m not against it, but it’s a sadness anytime you lose something.

272
00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:46.408
<v MUNGIN>Alright.

273
00:18:46.408 --> 00:18:47.225
Last question.

274
00:18:47.225 --> 00:18:54.574
What’s the most important thing you want people to remember about Mossville that isn’t in the history books?

275
00:18:54.574 --> 00:19:02.332
<v FONDEL>Well, the most important thing I want people to remember is that when you drive down Old Spanish Trail,

276
00:19:02.332 --> 00:19:10.907
you may . . . This community center may still be here, the swimming pool and all that, but there were people here too.

277
00:19:10.907 --> 00:19:14.173
A lot of African American people were here.

278
00:19:14.173 --> 00:19:21.931
And I think this is one of the communities that survived after slavery after . . . During Reconstruction and all that.

279
00:19:21.931 --> 00:19:25.197
So I just . . . In between Westlake and Sulphur,

280
00:19:25.197 --> 00:19:34.180
because they did have an African American . . . It didn’t grow as big as some of the other communities, but they were here.

281
00:19:34.180 --> 00:19:40.713
And one of these days when you drive down here, you’re going to see a fence.

282
00:19:40.713 --> 00:19:49.696
An eight foot fence with that wire, that razor wire all around it, but at one time they had people living there.

283
00:19:49.696 --> 00:19:53.370
And that’s not a bad thing I don’t think.

284
00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:59.495
I’m not trying to say it’s a bad thing, but it is what it is.

285
00:19:59.495 --> 00:20:15.010
And hopefully that some of these jobs that they bringing in will trickle down to some of the people living in this area because one thing that I’ve noticed . . . I’m not trying to point no finger at nobody.

286
00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:25.218
I don’t know why, but a lot of times when economic boom happens, it don’t necessarily happen for the people that it affect the most.

287
00:20:25.218 --> 00:20:28.484
And that’s the sadness that I see in

288
00:20:28.484 --> 00:20:28.893
<c.p>it.

289
00:20:28.893 --> 00:20:35.834
Now it may be because that person didn’t assert his self to get that job or whatever,

290
00:20:35.834 --> 00:20:41.550
but that’s the only thing that I pray that we all prosper from this.

291
00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:44.000
Not just a select few.

292
00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:52.039
<v MUNGIN>Alright well our time is almost up and on behalf of the center and the museum, we appreciate your contribution today.

293
00:20:52.039 --> 00:20:52.422
<v FONDEL>Okay.

294
00:20:52.422 --> 00:20:56.250
<v MUNGIN>Do you have anything you want to add in closing?

295
00:20:56.250 --> 00:20:57.781
<v FONDEL>No, that’s about it.

296
00:20:57.781 --> 00:21:02.375
Y’all can strike that part about driving home with the over [laughs].

297
00:21:02.375 --> 00:21:05.438
<v MUNGIN>We’re going to definitely keep that part in.

298
00:21:05.438 --> 00:21:06.203
<v FONDEL>It’s autopilot.

299
00:21:06.203 --> 00:21:07.734
Put in autopilot [laughs].

300
00:21:07.734 --> 00:21:09.648
No, but no I’m good.

301
00:21:09.648 --> 00:21:10.031
<v MUNGIN>Alright.

302
00:21:10.031 --> 00:21:13.477
<v FONDEL>And I want to thank y’all for doing this.

303
00:21:13.477 --> 00:21:14.625
I appreciate it.

304
00:21:14.625 --> 00:21:24.578
I’m glad y’all come down here to try to capture some of this because listening to my father, he was like a historian in our family.

305
00:21:24.578 --> 00:21:33.000
A lot of stories that he had . . . Sometimes a lot of stuff gets lost, and your history is one of them.

306
00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:33.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4400.

307
00:21:33.429 --> 00:21:33.857
End Session I.]
